  "worked_solution": "The linearity property of the Laplace transform states that for functions $f(t)$ and $g(t),$ we have\n\n$$\nL{\\alpha f(t)+\\beta g(t)}=\\alpha \\cdot L{f(t)}+\\beta \\cdot L{g(t)},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants.\n\nTaking the Laplace transform and applying the linearity property, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{3-5{e}^{2t}} & =L{3}+L{-5{e}^{2t}} \\\\  & =3L{1}-5L{{e}^{2t}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we recall the following result:\n\n$$\nL{{e}^{kt}}=\\frac{1}{s-k},\\,s>k\n$$\n\n$$\nL{1}=\\frac{1}{s},\\,s>0\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{1} & =\\frac{1}{s},\\,s>0 \\\\ L{{e}^{2t}} & =\\frac{1}{s-2},\\,s>2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Laplace transform of $f(t)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{f(t)} & =3L{1}-5L{{e}^{2t}} \\\\  & =3\\cdot \\frac{1}{s}-5\\cdot \\frac{1}{s-2} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{s}-\\frac{5}{s-2} \\\\  & =\\frac{3(s-2)-5s}{s(s-2)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2(s+3)}{s(s-2)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since $L{1}$ is defined for $s\\in (0,\\infty )$ and $L{{e}^{2t}}$ is defined for $s\\in (2,\\infty ),$ we have that $L{f(t)}$ is defined for\n\n$$\n(0,\\infty )\\cap (2,\\infty )=(2,\\infty ),\n$$\n\nthat is, $s>2.$",
